// Practice quiz: Writing and running script files
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: python/python-fundamentals/writing-and-running-script-files

::o1 run a script::On macOS or Linux, which command runs the script report.py in the current folder? {
=python3 report.py #Correct. Pass the file to the interpreter; on Windows the usual command is py report.py.
~java report.py #Not quite. java runs Java programs, not Python scripts.
~import report.py #Not quite. import is a Python statement, not a shell command.
~python3 -m report.py #Not quite. -m takes a module name without .py.
}

::o1 exit status::A script ends with sys.exit(1). Right after it, what does echo $? print in bash? {
=1 #Correct. The value passed to sys.exit becomes the process exit status.
~0 #Not quite. 0 would mean success; this script exited with 1.
~True #Not quite. The status is a number.
~Nothing, because Python has exited #Not quite. The shell still reports the last command's status.
}

::o1 success status::What does an exit status of 0 mean to a shell or CI job? {
=The command succeeded #Correct. By convention 0 is success and any other value signals failure.
~The command failed #Not quite. Failure is signalled by a non-zero status.
~The script printed nothing #Not quite. The status is separate from the output.
~The script had zero arguments #Not quite. The status says nothing about arguments.
}

::o2 name when run::You run python3 tools.py. What is __name__ inside tools.py? {
="__main__" #Correct. The file run as the program gets the name __main__.
~"tools" #Not quite. That is its name only when another file imports it.
~"tools.py" #Not quite. __name__ never includes the .py extension.
~None #Not quite. __name__ is always set.
}

::o2 guard on import::test_tools.py runs import tools, and tools.py ends with if __name__ \=\= "__main__"\: main(). Does main() run during the import? {
=No, because __name__ is "tools" on import #Correct. The guard is False when the module is imported, so the test can import it safely.
~Yes, every top-level line runs on import #Not quite. The if line runs, but its condition is False, so main() is skipped.
~Yes, but only once #Not quite. It does not run at all on import.
~The import fails #Not quite. Guarded files import normally.
}

::o3 argv contents::python3 thumbs.py album 800. What is sys.argv[2]? {
='800', a string #Correct. Item 0 is thumbs.py, item 1 album, item 2 '800'; every item is a string.
~800, an int #Not quite. sys.argv items are always strings; use int() to convert.
~'album' #Not quite. That is item 1.
~IndexError #Not quite. There are three items, so index 2 exists.
}

::o3 missing argument::A script needs exactly one argument. Which check reports a missing argument and exits with a failure status? {
=if len(sys.argv) !\= 2\: print the usage message to stderr, then sys.exit(2) #Correct. sys.argv always holds the script name first, so exactly one argument means 2 items; a non-zero status signals failure.
~if sys.argv[1] is None\: sys.exit(0) #Not quite. Reading sys.argv[1] when it is missing raises IndexError, and status 0 means success.
~if len(sys.argv) \=\= 0\: sys.exit(2) #Not quite. sys.argv always holds at least the script name, so this is never true.
~try\: argv \= sys.argv except\: pass #Not quite. This never detects the missing argument.
}
